00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
when the attorney general swella  braverman announced that she was  expecting her  second child a curious problem emerged  under current laws she would have to  resign to take time off to have a child  now unlike most workplaces in this  country  her position doesn't come with maternity  leave  a special bill has been drawn up to try  and fix the outdated laws  uh very pleased to say uh the women's  rights activist dr helen  helen pankers which he leads the  centenary action group which campaigns  for women's representation and  participation in politics  and she joins us on the programme helen  great to have you on and  and no prizes for any of our viewers who  recognize that very famous  surname of yours but but let's perhaps  start with with the 21st century  or indeed aspects of the 19th century  that still appear to be plaguing the  house of commons what do you make of the  the current rules around maternity  well ridiculous arcane i mean whatever  critical term you could be using i mean  it's great  that the government is introducing this  bill the problem is that it's only  resolving the problem for some people so  for the secretary of state and shadow  cabinet  ministers what we need is  a systematic uniform automatic  right for paternity adoption  maternity leave all of that so that  whole  hr system that ensures equality  and that ensures that all mps can do  their job  and have cover some systems some  resources  when they are away on leave  i i just wonder why you feel there has  perhaps been this  reticence to deal with a problem that  frankly should have been obvious  to anyone with eyes i mean i remember  very well  at the eruptions that went on and in  private  you know when it was proposed that one  of the bars get changed into a crash  you know it does feel like parliament  sometimes is languishing  you know back in the days of your great  grandmother  yeah it does take us back centuries  really doesn't it  it's such a pity i mean you know we want  to have more  uh it we want citizens and mps to be  more in touch with each other and  understand the issues that are  um that are in society and we want to  increase the percentage of  women in parliament uh you know  currently it's what it's  34 and in terms of counsellors it's 35  so we we want to get that equality and  we want more diversity so  parliament to also represent all the  diversity bame issues of disability  for it to be a place where anybody can  thrive  and within this coalition that i'm  involved in the centenary action group  we are campaigning to look at and  address the barriers but also  to get more people that come up and say  they want to become  mps they want to become counsellors and  when they see  that ludicrous um  i mean i'm i'm losing the words but the  system the hr system  that we have is just not fit for purpose  it's not fit for the 21st  century and we have this opportunity  right now  and it you know it's sad in a way that  it took  one of their own it took a uh secretary  of state to  be pregnant for the governments to now  want to rush through a  uh a policy and what we need to make  sure is that this policy isn't just for  some  that actually it addresses that the  absolute need  for you know structural systems that  ensure that  any uh mp can work  effectively when they are pregnant and  they need to take leave  that their constituencies are well  served that there's a good system  that ensures that a citizen has a system  that continues and that is represented  and that's what mps are calling for  that's what we need to ensure happens  yeah i mean i mean i suppose the kind of  the concomitant effect of there having  been no kind of maternity cover is that  you know  parliamentarians have generally tended  not to have children now if that were to  change in significant numbers  we would need to see some tinkering with  the system wouldn't we i mean  the job of an mp is not quite the same  as any other role  when an mp is offer an extended period  you need someone in there with the same  level of authority to deal with  constituents  to deal with votes in the house and and  so on  exactly and it's about having a system  because otherwise what happens  is for whatever reason uh you know  a father who wants to take time off uh  there's this guilt that and it's not uh  it's not  best practice and we know that if you  have a system it's  known it's transparent then people will  use it  they'll be able to work effectively what  we have now  is a parliament where women cannot work  effectively  when they are pregnant when they need to  take leave fathers can't do so either  either  when there's issues of sickness as well  there's no transparent automatic system  um so it ends up being a little bit a  rule for one and not the other and  that's just really not what we need it  it's just not effective it's not  efficient  it's not equitable dr helen pankas great  to have you on the program this morning  really enjoyed talking to you thanks for  being here  thank you

